ding the city of Carthage during the Roman siege."}, {"sourceSlug": "himilco-fameas", "sourceName": "Himilco Fameas", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "roman-military-advantage", "targetName": "Roman Military Advantage", "context": "Himilco Phameas's defection directly contributed to a significant Roman military advantage during the final stages of the Third Punic War."}], "places": [{"name": "Carthage, Modern Tunisia", "role": "Siege Defense"}, {"name": "North Africa, Ancient Carthage", "role": "Theater of Operations"}, {"name": "Rome, Ancient Italy", "role": "Opposing Power"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Himilco Fameas", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "himilco-fameas"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3796632", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phameas"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Himilco Phameas's defection dramatically altered the balance of power in the Third Punic War, directly aiding Scipio Aemilianus in the final destruction of Carthage. His actions serve as a powerful case study in military opportunism and its regional impact.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-17T16:33:20Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29596440701", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Phameas, sometimes also known as Hamilcar or Himilco Phameas, was a Carthaginian officer during the Third Punic War.", "newValue": "Himilco Phameas, a Carthaginian cavalry commander active during the crucial Third Punic War (149–146 BCE), represents a pivotal example of military defection and strategic opportunism that significantly impacted the war's trajectory. Originally a formidable opponent of Rome, his shifting loyalties and tactical prowess played a direct role in the early Roman setbacks before his eventual, decisive alliance with Scipio Aemilianus. His career highlights the internal divisions and external pressures facing Carthage in its final struggle against the ascendant Roman Republic, ultimately contributing to the city's destruction.

Phameas first distinguished himself in 149 BCE by inflicting considerable damage on Roman forces during their initial siege attempts on Carthage. Leading Carthaginian cavalry, he successfully harassed Roman supply lines, ambushed foraging parties, and maintained a mobile defense that frustrated consular commanders Manius Manilius and Lucius Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus. His tactical acumen was particularly evident in skirmishes outside the city walls, where he leveraged the terrain to his advantage, making him one of the most effective Carthaginian field commanders. However, observing the overwhelming Roman commitment and the internal political struggles within Carthage, Phameas initiated secret negotiations with the Roman general Scipio Aemilianus.

